1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,300
It's that metallic stuff that glistens in the sun with an unrivaled brilliant reflection.

2
00:00:06,301 --> 00:00:09,300
If there's no chrome in heaven, I'm not going.

3
00:00:09,301 --> 00:00:12,400
But don't let its flashy facade fool you.

4
00:00:12,401 --> 00:00:18,640
It also protects steel, stops corrosion, and transforms plastic.

5
00:00:18,641 --> 00:00:24,720
Whether restoring a classic or tricking out a truck, this metal shines above the rest.

6
00:00:24,721 --> 00:00:28,000
Girls like diamonds, guys like chrome.

7
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:33,000
From the gleaming top of the Chrysler Building to life-sized sparkling stallions.

8
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:37,000
Now, Chrome on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Chrome. Even the word sounds lustrous.

10
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:56,560
For some, it's a subtle detail.

11
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:58,720
For others, a bold statement.

12
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:01,840
But for hot rod enthusiast Mike Heron,

13
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,120
chrome is pretty much everything.

14
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:12,340
I don't think you can have enough chrome on a car.

15
00:01:12,341 --> 00:01:15,820
I think the chrome is the whole heart of the car.

16
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:22,160
This is a 1943 Packard Dash 5 2500 Flyer.

17
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:24,580
Probably 80% of the car is chrome.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,220
The very front is my favorite part of the car.

19
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,940
It's out of a tractor trailer.

20
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:31,160
It's a drop front end.

21
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:32,360
It's all chrome plated.

22
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:34,080
The calipers are chrome plated.

23
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:35,820
The disc brakes are plated.

24
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:38,480
The front end, the drag links, the headlights.

25
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:40,640
It's probably the most dramatic part of the car.

26
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:48,940
Only the 30-foot-long body of the 16,000-pound metal Roadster lacks the true luster of chrome.

27
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:52,780
The car itself is all aluminum.

28
00:01:52,781 --> 00:01:55,160
and then we polished it out to make it look like chrome

29
00:01:55,161 --> 00:01:57,920
because chrome has to be put into tanks

30
00:01:57,921 --> 00:01:59,620
and then it's electroplated.

31
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:02,880
So they don't make tanks large enough to put the whole car in.

32
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:05,060
If we could get it in the tanks, we would have plated it.

33
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:08,600
Amazingly, there's not really much chrome on the car.

34
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:12,660
The chrome plating on the parts is just millions of an inch thick.

35
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:14,800
But more on that later.

36
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:18,840
Perhaps the most dazzling part of the chrome mobile

37
00:02:18,841 --> 00:02:20,820
is its fire-breathing engine.

38
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:31,580
The engine was originally out of a 1943 PT boat, and we converted it and built this entire

39
00:02:31,581 --> 00:02:34,847
car around it. And it's the ultimate chrome machine.

40
00:02:34,848 --> 00:02:36,920
It puts out 3,000 horsepower, and

41
00:02:36,921 --> 00:02:41,113
it's 2,500 cubic inches. The fastest we've had is about 125.

42
00:02:41,114 --> 00:02:42,860
Still had a lot of power.

43
00:02:42,861 --> 00:02:45,660
It was still climbing. We were just not sure we could stop it.

44
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,740
Mike spent more than a million dollars custom building the gleaming chrome beast.

45
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:56,460
I think it's everything.

46
00:02:56,461 --> 00:02:59,740
I think it adds luster, you know, a mystery to the car.

47
00:02:59,741 --> 00:03:03,740
It just makes the car.

48
00:03:03,741 --> 00:03:08,600
So just what is this mysterious gleaming substance we call chrome?

49
00:03:08,601 --> 00:03:13,860
Chrome is short for chromium, which is a highly reflective metal resistant to tarnish and

50
00:03:13,861 --> 00:03:14,860
corrosion.

51
00:03:14,861 --> 00:03:22,760
That's because upon contact with the atmosphere, the metal develops a protective oxide coating.

52
00:03:22,761 --> 00:03:25,100
It's just like iron rust, but clear.

53
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:30,860
Perhaps nobody depends on chrome's corrosion resistance

54
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:33,000
and bright sheen more than truckers.

55
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,720
At four state trucks in Joplin, Missouri,

56
00:03:38,721 --> 00:03:40,740
tens of thousands of truckers make their way

57
00:03:40,741 --> 00:03:42,140
to this treasure trove of chrome.

58
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:48,920
It's the home of the self-proclaimed Chrome Shop Mafia.

59
00:03:52,760 --> 00:04:01,260
Truckers go 40, 50, sometimes 100 miles out of route to come by here, visit the store,

60
00:04:01,261 --> 00:04:04,840
and get their chrome fix. Whether it's a little gadget they want for their dash,

61
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:08,720
you know, a nice shiny front bumper, even these big exhaust pipes we sell in stock,

62
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:11,840
they've got to have their chrome. They're addicted to chrome sometimes.

63
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:20,840
At their on-site installation room, this 2007 Peterbilt 379 is in the middle of an extreme chrome makeover.

64
00:04:20,841 --> 00:04:30,221
We have a few finishing touches to put on it yet, such as a chrome-plated .22 to a .20 tapered bumper, an exhaust stack, some chrome-plated plastic hub covers, a

65
00:04:30,222 --> 00:04:32,202
We're almost done chroming it out.

66
00:04:32,203 --> 00:04:38,320
chrome spot mirror on the mirrors up here by the door, and just various little knick-knacky stuff inside.

67
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:45,380
Swapping out the bumper and adding minor chrome accents takes little more than a few nuts and bolts.

68
00:04:45,381 --> 00:04:55,820
but attaching the nine foot tall 100 pound chrome exhaust stack requires some real elbow grease we

69
00:04:55,821 --> 00:05:00,920
made our own t-pipe it's 10 inches as well as the pipe is and we're trying to slide this pipe into

70
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:04,940
our t-pipe it's not the easiest thing in the world to maneuver because it's so big and awkward and

71
00:05:04,941 --> 00:05:09,440
heavy but it fills up this entire gap so you don't have any open cavities on your truck so

72
00:05:09,441 --> 00:05:11,000
So it's well worth it in the end.

73
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:17,980
Of course, there's always a cheaper alternative to chrome.

74
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:21,620
But that means sacrificing the unique glint of the metal.

75
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:27,520
On these big rigs, there's a lot of chrome on it, but there's also a lot of stainless steel.

76
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:29,140
And from a distance, they're all shiny.

77
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:30,440
They're all somewhat reflective.

78
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:32,340
But here's something for comparison.

79
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:35,360
This is just a raw piece of stainless steel.

80
00:05:35,361 --> 00:05:39,380
and we can take the yardstick, kind of hold it up there and look into it like a mirror.

81
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:43,080
You can see the image there, but it's a little foggy, a little blurry.

82
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:46,160
Now take a look at the chrome-plated finish down here on this bumper.

83
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:48,720
I mean, look at that.

84
00:05:48,721 --> 00:05:51,820
You can read every number right down to the hash marks, you know?

85
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:54,420
That's a true mirror image.

86
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:55,900
That's a good thing about chrome plating.

87
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,960
But chrome's origins are not so flashy.

88
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:05,080
The metal can be traced back to late 3rd century B.C. Chinese weapons.

89
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:12,360
Although buried more than 2,000 years ago, ancient bronze tips of swords found at the

90
00:06:12,361 --> 00:06:18,340
Terracotta Army burial pits showed no sign of corrosion.

91
00:06:18,341 --> 00:06:23,040
Scientists discovered the ancient weapon makers unknowingly created a bronze that contained

92
00:06:23,041 --> 00:06:26,220
2% chromium.

93
00:06:26,221 --> 00:06:29,840
Probably fires in the mausoleum caused the chromium to migrate to the surface of the

94
00:06:29,841 --> 00:06:33,160
swords.

95
00:06:33,161 --> 00:06:39,340
But it wasn't until 1797 that French chemist Nicolas-Louis Vacalin first discovered the

96
00:06:39,341 --> 00:06:43,560
lustrous metal in a red lead ore from Siberia.

97
00:06:43,561 --> 00:06:49,220
He named it chromium after the Greek word chroma, meaning color, since many of its compounds

98
00:06:49,221 --> 00:06:52,420
produce vibrant colors.

99
00:06:52,421 --> 00:06:58,920
And for more than a century, chromium's primary use was in paints and dyes.

100
00:06:58,921 --> 00:07:04,800
It wasn't until the early 1900s that metallurgists discovered chrome's corrosion resistance in

101
00:07:04,801 --> 00:07:06,040
the production of steel.

102
00:07:06,041 --> 00:07:10,540
Chromium is very important in the manufacturing of stainless steel.

103
00:07:10,541 --> 00:07:15,740
If it wasn't for the addition of chrome, stainless steel would no longer be stain resistant.

104
00:07:15,741 --> 00:07:22,860
Today, metal alloys account for 85% of chromium use.

105
00:07:22,861 --> 00:07:29,940
Almost 50% of the primary commercial ore, called chromite, is produced in South Africa.

106
00:07:29,941 --> 00:07:36,040
The ore is also abundant in India, Russia, Kazakhstan, and Turkey.

107
00:07:36,041 --> 00:07:42,300
In 2008, world production of chromium reached 15 million tons.

108
00:07:42,301 --> 00:07:46,900
That's $2.5 billion worth.

109
00:07:46,901 --> 00:07:50,140
The flashiest use of chrome is plating it onto other metals.

110
00:07:50,141 --> 00:07:56,040
In fact, objects are never made of solid chrome, but rather covered in a thin layer of the metal.

111
00:07:57,620 --> 00:08:00,060
So why not make a solid chrome bumper?

112
00:08:01,140 --> 00:08:08,240
Ultimately, it wouldn't be any shinier or more durable, but would add unnecessary weight and more than double the price.

113
00:08:09,060 --> 00:08:12,260
We now return to Chrome on Modern Marvels.

114
00:08:12,261 --> 00:08:21,020
At the Arlington Plating Company in Palatine, Illinois, 50 technicians chrome plate more

115
00:08:21,021 --> 00:08:26,740
than 15,000 aluminum and magnesium parts each week.

116
00:08:26,741 --> 00:08:31,260
To get the chrome to stick to the parts, technicians turn to electroplating.

117
00:08:31,261 --> 00:08:36,700
It's the process of coating an electrically conductive object with a layer of metal using

118
00:08:36,701 --> 00:08:38,420
direct current.

119
00:08:38,421 --> 00:08:43,420
For most metals, the process is pretty much the same.

120
00:08:43,421 --> 00:08:46,420
We run the current off a rectifier.

121
00:08:46,421 --> 00:08:52,420
What the rectifier will do, the rectifier will take AC current and transform it to DC current,

122
00:08:52,421 --> 00:08:55,420
like a giant battery, a car battery or something.

123
00:08:55,421 --> 00:09:00,420
You have your positive look up to both sides here, which is your anode.

124
00:09:00,421 --> 00:09:05,420
Your cathode or your part itself is the negative.

125
00:09:05,421 --> 00:09:11,500
The hanging baskets on each positively charged side contain the metal to be plated to the

126
00:09:11,501 --> 00:09:12,500
part.

127
00:09:12,501 --> 00:09:21,080
The 2,500-gallon tank is filled with an acidic solution that permits the flow of electricity.

128
00:09:21,081 --> 00:09:25,480
Since the part itself is negatively charged, it attracts the positively charged metal from

129
00:09:25,481 --> 00:09:29,440
the baskets.

130
00:09:29,441 --> 00:09:33,980
But creating a chrome shiny finish isn't just a quick dip.

131
00:09:33,981 --> 00:09:39,220
In their large-scale electroplating line, truck bumpers and step tubes travel back and

132
00:09:39,221 --> 00:09:43,820
forth for more than three hours between 28 tanks.

133
00:09:43,821 --> 00:09:48,900
The process begins with a 30-minute pre-treatment of several hot cleaners and a nitric acid

134
00:09:48,901 --> 00:09:54,780
blend to ensure the purest and smoothest aluminum surface.

135
00:09:54,781 --> 00:09:58,800
And before the bumper can get its splashy coat of chrome, it goes through four tanks

136
00:09:58,801 --> 00:10:01,080
of nickel for more than two hours.

137
00:10:01,081 --> 00:10:09,720
If I was just to put the chrome on there, it would not look shiny and beautiful and bright like everybody thinks chrome is.

138
00:10:09,721 --> 00:10:12,960
When we pull it out after the nickel tank, it looks yellow.

139
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:16,780
And if you put it out on a vehicle, it'll tarnish very quickly.

140
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:20,040
The chrome gives it that nice blue depth to it.

141
00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:24,060
And it allows, obviously, for it to last quite some time out in the field.

142
00:10:24,061 --> 00:10:29,820
While the aluminum bumper is just over an eighth of an inch thick, the nickel layer

143
00:10:29,821 --> 00:10:35,300
is only 15 ten-thousandths of an inch thick.

144
00:10:35,301 --> 00:10:39,060
And the outer chrome layer is just 15 millionths of an inch thick.

145
00:10:39,061 --> 00:10:45,740
That's more than 250 times thinner than a human hair.

146
00:10:45,741 --> 00:10:52,780
Even so, it adds a sparkle and the utmost resistance to corrosion.

147
00:10:52,781 --> 00:10:59,360
To test this resistance, Arlington Plating Company technicians turn to a salt spray chamber.

148
00:10:59,361 --> 00:11:04,860
Placing parts in the chamber for just eight hours simulates more than two years of real-life use.

149
00:11:04,861 --> 00:11:10,360
This is an unplated part, just raw steel part.

150
00:11:10,361 --> 00:11:13,540
As you can see it rusted, the iron rusted, forming an iron oxide.

151
00:11:13,541 --> 00:11:16,540
This is a nickel plated part, slight rusting, a little bit of yellow.

152
00:11:16,541 --> 00:11:18,180
And then we have the chrome plated parts.

153
00:11:18,181 --> 00:11:22,940
As you can see, virtually flawless finish, no visible rusting at all.

154
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:29,140
Chrome actually does rust like all other metals, but at a much slower rate.

155
00:11:30,140 --> 00:11:34,020
And unlike the eyesore of iron oxide, chrome oxide is clear.

156
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:41,440
Back at the chrome plating line, the part finally makes its way to the chrome tank,

157
00:11:41,441 --> 00:11:44,080
which requires a unique electroplating technique.

158
00:11:44,081 --> 00:11:49,480
We're here at a final step, which is the actual chrome process itself.

159
00:11:49,481 --> 00:11:52,080
The chrome here is actually in the solution.

160
00:11:52,081 --> 00:11:53,380
There are no chrome anodes.

161
00:11:53,381 --> 00:11:58,080
We use lead anodes to give us the conductivity and the throw we need to place the chrome

162
00:11:58,081 --> 00:11:59,080
on the part.

163
00:11:59,081 --> 00:12:04,780
Finally, after just one minute in the chrome tank, and then a few dunks in water to rinse

164
00:12:04,781 --> 00:12:10,080
off the golden chrome solution, this truck bumper is ready to hit the road.

165
00:12:10,081 --> 00:12:17,960
But not all chrome plating is so shiny and decorative.

166
00:12:17,961 --> 00:12:22,380
Hard chrome plating is applied directly to an object to create a thicker, more protective

167
00:12:22,381 --> 00:12:23,380
outer layer.

168
00:12:23,381 --> 00:12:30,260
While the chrome is still metallic, it's not very reflective, since there's no nickel

169
00:12:30,261 --> 00:12:31,260
substrate.

170
00:12:31,261 --> 00:12:38,260
The hard comb is used more in functional coating for wear, for lubricity, for hardness.

171
00:12:38,261 --> 00:12:45,060
Now, a part in hard chrome could be in a chrome tank, actually, for as long as hours.

172
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:51,640
At Arlington Plating, motorcycle pistons spend 10 minutes in the chrome tank,

173
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:57,000
creating a layer of metal that is 500 times thicker than the chrome on decorative parts.

174
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:02,480
Of course, most bikers aren't satisfied with just the duller hard chrome parts.

175
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:05,820
They want the shiny stuff, too.

176
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:13,700
This is a ultra classic full dresser, bagger they call them.

177
00:13:13,701 --> 00:13:20,800
I put chrome legs, I put chrome wheels, I change the calipers, I change the brake discs, all chrome.

178
00:13:20,801 --> 00:13:23,680
So when it's rolling, it's shining.

179
00:13:23,681 --> 00:13:26,940
It just glitters no matter what you do.

180
00:13:26,941 --> 00:13:27,940
It's like a ring.

181
00:13:27,941 --> 00:13:30,940
Girls like diamonds, guys like chrome.

182
00:13:30,941 --> 00:13:37,060
Chromium is an essential component in the green ink used to produce paper money in the United States.

183
00:13:37,061 --> 00:13:44,260
Not only does the chromium create the green pigment, it also prevents the ink from being erased or copied.

184
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:48,440
Chrome will return on Modern Marvels.

185
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:52,880
We now return to Chrome on Modern Marvels.

186
00:13:54,840 --> 00:14:00,660
For more than a century, chromium has captivated us as one of the most luminous metals found on Earth.

187
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:04,840
Sure, it's all over big rigs and motorcycles.

188
00:14:04,841 --> 00:14:11,620
But when it comes to chrome flash, nothing beats the shiny display of eye candy at a classic car show.

189
00:14:11,940 --> 00:14:14,640
This is a 1953 Cadillac Coupe DeVille.

190
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:17,180
It has quite a large amount of chrome on it.

191
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:20,260
It's just a wonderful car to own. It attracts a lot of attention.

192
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:31,700
In Santa Ana, California, more than 200 car enthusiasts gather each month to pay homage to the golden, or should we say, silver age of chrome.

193
00:14:31,701 --> 00:14:36,500
It actually started in 1926 with the first car.

194
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:38,740
It was a 1926 Oldsmobile,

195
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:42,620
and it became a marketing tool in the designer's features

196
00:14:42,621 --> 00:14:46,520
because originally they had brass, they had copper.

197
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:50,060
Well, all of that would tarnish and would tarnish very quickly.

198
00:14:51,620 --> 00:14:54,440
Soon, automakers used the metal to adorn everything

199
00:14:54,441 --> 00:14:56,500
from the front bumper to the taillights.

200
00:14:57,880 --> 00:15:01,620
The superior corrosion resistance and unmatched polish of chrome

201
00:15:01,621 --> 00:15:03,320
caught on with consumers.

202
00:15:04,780 --> 00:15:09,080
By the 1950s, chrome, virtually unknown 30 years earlier,

203
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:12,940
had become a household word and a standard in car design.

204
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:18,480
Case in point, this 1954 Packard Caribbean.

205
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:20,580
At the time they designed this car,

206
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:22,640
what they were looking for is sales,

207
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:26,080
and the chrome basically sold the car.

208
00:15:26,760 --> 00:15:28,760
If you removed all the chrome and painted all this,

209
00:15:28,761 --> 00:15:32,900
it would actually have not probably resulted in a lot of sales.

210
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:40,800
The use of this aptly named bright work peaked in 1958

211
00:15:40,801 --> 00:15:43,340
with the Oldsmobile 98 sedan,

212
00:15:44,120 --> 00:15:48,340
believed to have more chrome per square inch than any other car from the decade.

213
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:55,520
While grinning wide-mouthed grills and soaring tail fins may dazzle with their bold sparkle,

214
00:15:55,521 --> 00:16:01,620
Perhaps the most intricate and unique chrome piece on a classic car is the hood ornament,

215
00:16:01,621 --> 00:16:05,020
or mascot as collectors call them.

216
00:16:05,021 --> 00:16:12,400
At the American Aero Corporation near Detroit, Michigan, classic car collector and restoration

217
00:16:12,401 --> 00:16:20,080
expert Don Sommer preserves more than 3,000 sparkling chrome mascots.

218
00:16:20,081 --> 00:16:23,580
Most of the miniature chrome sculptures in Sommer's collection are worth a few hundred

219
00:16:23,581 --> 00:16:29,460
dollars apiece. Others are so unique they're valued in the thousands.

220
00:16:29,461 --> 00:16:34,916
The most rare mascot that I have out here on the table is this boat.

221
00:16:34,917 --> 00:16:36,200
This is the only

222
00:16:36,201 --> 00:16:37,997
one known in the world.

223
00:16:37,998 --> 00:16:42,840
Looks like sort of a takeoff of a PT boat and another rare one

224
00:16:42,841 --> 00:16:45,928
here is this Packard radio antenna.

225
00:16:45,929 --> 00:16:50,340
Very few of these exist and this particular one is

226
00:16:50,341 --> 00:16:55,620
set up to be used on a 3940 Packard and that was an actual antenna. I think I

227
00:16:55,621 --> 00:17:01,500
paid $2,500 at the time for it. That was probably 25 years ago.

228
00:17:01,501 --> 00:17:07,200
Mascots weren't always so shiny and ornate. In fact they began as functional

229
00:17:07,201 --> 00:17:11,460
temperature meters mounted to the radiator caps on the front of cars.

230
00:17:11,461 --> 00:17:16,500
Here's an example of one that was on a Cleveland car and these are called

231
00:17:16,501 --> 00:17:21,460
motor meters and and they and they told you what the temperature was in the radiator

232
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:27,300
when automakers moved the temperature gauge to the dash in the late 1920s

233
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:31,860
they replaced the moto meter with ornaments to cover up the humble radiator cap

234
00:17:33,460 --> 00:17:38,500
and it became the mark of the car and so they started to have a contest to see who could get

235
00:17:38,501 --> 00:17:45,060
the prettiest ones by the 1930s elaborate chrome plated hood ornaments were standard

236
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:51,500
It wasn't until 1958 that automakers started removing the chrome accents,

237
00:17:52,020 --> 00:17:55,440
since they posed a high risk for injuries and collisions with pedestrians.

238
00:17:58,100 --> 00:18:00,180
Beyond collecting the chrome figurines,

239
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:04,780
Summer also has been creating and selling replicas of them for more than 40 years.

240
00:18:07,280 --> 00:18:10,200
Out of the 230 chrome mascots he offers,

241
00:18:11,020 --> 00:18:13,500
the Pierce Harrow Archer shines above the rest.

242
00:18:13,501 --> 00:18:16,960
I make it in two pieces, the cap and the ornament separate.

243
00:18:17,340 --> 00:18:20,360
When it gets all done, it's about $800 for the combination.

244
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:23,360
To recreate the chrome archer,

245
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:26,180
Summer utilizes the lost wax method.

246
00:18:27,100 --> 00:18:30,500
It starts at his shop with a meticulously shaped wax mold.

247
00:18:32,080 --> 00:18:34,220
From there, the mold heads to a foundry,

248
00:18:34,680 --> 00:18:36,060
where it's cast in stucco.

249
00:18:36,940 --> 00:18:39,900
It was developed in ancient times for statuary,

250
00:18:39,901 --> 00:18:44,560
but it's a way you can make some very complicated castings

251
00:18:44,561 --> 00:18:46,640
and when you say lost wax,

252
00:18:46,900 --> 00:18:48,960
it's a process by which you lose the wax.

253
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:52,240
After melting the wax out,

254
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:55,660
workers pour molten stainless steel into the stucco shell.

255
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,300
Once cooled down and knocked from its shell,

256
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:02,780
the stainless steel mascot is polished

257
00:19:02,781 --> 00:19:05,780
and then moves on to a local chrome plating shop.

258
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:09,700
There, workers flash-plated for just 15 seconds

259
00:19:09,701 --> 00:19:19,600
in chrome to give it that desirable sheen.

260
00:19:19,601 --> 00:19:26,460
The optimistic age of fins in chrome was cut short by the oil crisis of the 1970s.

261
00:19:26,461 --> 00:19:32,020
New emission regulations and fuel economy restrictions forced automakers to change their designs.

262
00:19:32,021 --> 00:19:38,180
And the heavy steel with chrome plating was kicked to the curb.

263
00:19:38,181 --> 00:19:41,340
took its place.

264
00:19:41,341 --> 00:19:43,880
But thanks to new electroplating technology,

265
00:19:43,881 --> 00:19:48,060
chrome is making a comeback in the form of plastic.

266
00:19:48,061 --> 00:19:49,860
Much like cars in the past that used

267
00:19:49,861 --> 00:19:52,640
a lot of decorative metal parts, vehicles today

268
00:19:52,641 --> 00:19:54,560
have a lot of plastic components that are

269
00:19:54,561 --> 00:19:56,360
chrome-plated on them.

270
00:19:56,361 --> 00:19:58,220
Starting out with this vehicle, we

271
00:19:58,221 --> 00:20:00,560
have a chrome-plated plastic grill.

272
00:20:00,561 --> 00:20:03,380
Along with the grill, we have emblems, or nameplates,

273
00:20:03,381 --> 00:20:07,340
as they're called, the skull cap on the mirror, door handles,

274
00:20:07,341 --> 00:20:09,340
as well as the body side moldings.

275
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:17,840
The 450,000 square foot SRG Global Plastic Chrome Plating Facility in Moorhead, Kentucky.

276
00:20:17,841 --> 00:20:21,840
Chromes as many as 15,000 plastic parts every day.

277
00:20:21,841 --> 00:20:26,840
Behind me is an injection molding machine, one of 28 we have here in the plant.

278
00:20:26,841 --> 00:20:29,340
This particular machine is molding a grill.

279
00:20:29,341 --> 00:20:31,840
The process starts with the bulk material.

280
00:20:31,841 --> 00:20:34,840
The bulk material is fed into the barrel of the machine.

281
00:20:34,841 --> 00:20:39,240
The barrel melts the material from bulk form into a molten form.

282
00:20:39,420 --> 00:20:44,800
When it's in a molten form, it gets shot or injected into the molding tool you see behind us.

283
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:53,440
Not only is chrome plating plastic more cost effective than using a steel substrate,

284
00:20:54,220 --> 00:20:57,420
but it's also lighter and ultimately more fuel efficient.

285
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:02,220
Before the molded parts can get chrome plated,

286
00:21:02,221 --> 00:21:07,940
It must undergo an acid etching process so that the surface of the plastic behaves like a metal.

287
00:21:09,380 --> 00:21:13,120
We're at the beginning part of the process here, which is called the etching phase.

288
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:21,580
At this part of the process is where we use a blend of chromic acid, sulfuric acid, along with heat to create frosty onto the surface of the part.

289
00:21:22,660 --> 00:21:29,040
The acid solution eats away at the plastic for about 10 minutes, creating microscopic craters on the part.

290
00:21:29,041 --> 00:21:36,440
Next, the sponge-like surface absorbs palladium to make up for plastic's inherently low static

291
00:21:36,441 --> 00:21:40,140
electric charge.

292
00:21:40,141 --> 00:21:44,440
The palladium deposits ultimately allow a chemical reaction to occur with nickel.

293
00:21:44,441 --> 00:21:49,840
The bath that we're in right now is called the Electrolous Nickel Bath.

294
00:21:49,841 --> 00:21:54,000
In this part of the process, the nickel now begins to react with that palladium and begins

295
00:21:54,001 --> 00:21:58,220
to deposit in those small little crevices and then grows out along the surface of the

296
00:21:58,221 --> 00:22:02,860
part. And now it begins to encapsulate it at about 10 to 12 millionths of an inch.

297
00:22:05,020 --> 00:22:08,700
Finally, the part is ready for its plunge into the chrome electroplating tank.

298
00:22:10,180 --> 00:22:16,960
The end result? A lightweight alternative to metal that's just as brilliant.

299
00:22:18,380 --> 00:22:21,880
And it's used on more than 7 million cars each year.

300
00:22:21,881 --> 00:22:30,180
In order to devote Chromium to the war effort, the United States government started regulating the use of the metal in 1942.

301
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:36,280
The resulting chrome-restricted cars were dubbed Blackout Specials.

302
00:22:37,580 --> 00:22:39,980
Chrome will return on Modern Marvels.

303
00:22:41,380 --> 00:22:44,880
We now return to Chrome on Modern Marvels.

304
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:50,320
Our houses are loaded with Chrome.

305
00:22:51,880 --> 00:23:00,920
But chrome-plated fixtures are just a small portion of the chrome in our homes.

306
00:23:00,921 --> 00:23:06,280
The metal's real dominance comes because of its ability to make steel stainless.

307
00:23:06,281 --> 00:23:11,440
In fact, almost 70% of all chrome is used in the production of stainless steel.

308
00:23:11,441 --> 00:23:18,440
And every year, the world demands about 27 million tons of stainless steel for cookware,

309
00:23:18,441 --> 00:23:21,300
cutlery, hardware, and major appliances.

310
00:23:23,120 --> 00:23:26,120
At North American Stainless in Gent, Kentucky,

311
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:30,600
more than 3,000 tons of the shiny steel is manufactured each day.

312
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:35,420
The process starts with the charging of scrap into the charging basket.

313
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:40,760
North American Stainless is probably one of the biggest recyclers of scrap in the United States.

314
00:23:40,761 --> 00:23:48,260
Every year we process about 780,000 tons of stainless scrap to manufacture a finished product.

315
00:23:48,440 --> 00:23:55,840
To boost the chrome level, workers turn to ferrochrome, an alloy of chrome and iron that

316
00:23:55,841 --> 00:24:01,980
contains between 45 and 65 percent chrome.

317
00:24:01,981 --> 00:24:08,000
It's added to the scrap to achieve at least 10.5 percent chromium content.

318
00:24:08,001 --> 00:24:12,200
Anything lower than that, and the resulting metal would not be stainless steel.

319
00:24:12,201 --> 00:24:18,380
Though it may not look the part, adding just two tons of this granular chrome mixture to

320
00:24:18,381 --> 00:24:29,120
each 150-ton batch of scrap ultimately adds luster and corrosion resistance to the steel.

321
00:24:29,121 --> 00:24:34,120
Once collected, an overhead crane dumps the raw materials into a massive furnace.

322
00:24:34,121 --> 00:24:48,121
After reaching a temperature of 2200 degrees Fahrenheit in 45 minutes, now molten metal

323
00:24:49,620 --> 00:24:51,620
heads to a refining vessel.

324
00:24:51,621 --> 00:24:57,440
We'll be blowing argon and oxygen into the bottom of the vessel to remove the impurities

325
00:24:57,441 --> 00:25:00,020
in the steel, carbon and sulfur.

326
00:25:00,021 --> 00:25:03,540
We'll stop midpoint and take a test sample from the vessel.

327
00:25:04,060 --> 00:25:06,640
And we'll be at that point checking the chrome content.

328
00:25:07,060 --> 00:25:12,900
If the chrome content is not as we want, we have an overhead alloy system where we add further chrome.

329
00:25:14,380 --> 00:25:18,400
While stainless steel may have as little as 10.5% chromium,

330
00:25:18,980 --> 00:25:24,180
this particular mixture, destined for flatware production, requires 18% chrome.

331
00:25:24,181 --> 00:25:31,180
The higher percentage of chromium is essential for the flatware to withstand a houseful of corrosive agents.

332
00:25:31,181 --> 00:25:36,180
Many of the household items that we take for granted are caustic, and they eat into steel.

333
00:25:36,181 --> 00:25:40,180
In this experiment, we took common household items such as vinegar,

334
00:25:40,181 --> 00:25:45,180
and we applied it to one sample, which was stainless steel, and the other a low-carbon steel.

335
00:25:45,181 --> 00:25:48,180
What we're doing is we're going to remove the vinegar from here,

336
00:25:48,181 --> 00:25:51,180
but as we remove it, we can see that there's no rust.

337
00:25:51,181 --> 00:25:57,180
In this, the low carbon steel, as we remove the vinegar,

338
00:25:57,181 --> 00:25:59,180
there's a different story.

339
00:25:59,181 --> 00:26:01,180
Because of the lack of chrome,

340
00:26:01,181 --> 00:26:03,180
we see the corrosion process begin.

341
00:26:06,180 --> 00:26:11,180
From wine, to soda, to bleach,

342
00:26:11,181 --> 00:26:13,180
the results are all the same.

343
00:26:13,181 --> 00:26:16,180
They never make stainless steel with more than 20% chrome

344
00:26:16,181 --> 00:26:19,180
because it doesn't add much more corrosion resistance.

345
00:26:19,181 --> 00:26:26,800
resistance, but significantly increases the cost.

346
00:26:26,801 --> 00:26:32,020
Once the molten steel reaches the specified chrome concentration, it's molded and then

347
00:26:32,021 --> 00:26:40,180
cut by 4,000-degree Fahrenheit torch flames into 35-ton, 43-foot-long semi-finished forms

348
00:26:40,181 --> 00:26:41,180
called slabs.

349
00:26:41,181 --> 00:26:46,340
They're brought to the top-mill furnace, which is behind me here.

350
00:26:46,341 --> 00:26:51,160
At that point in time they are placed into the furnace and they are in the furnace approximately

351
00:26:51,161 --> 00:26:58,400
two and a half hours to reheat those slabs to around 2,320 degrees Fahrenheit to reduce

352
00:26:58,401 --> 00:27:08,780
their strength so we're able to hot roll the product.

353
00:27:08,781 --> 00:27:15,060
The hot mill uses two rollers to compress the 43 foot long 8 inch thick slab to a 2,000

354
00:27:15,061 --> 00:27:22,300
put long coil, just under a fifth of an inch thick.

355
00:27:22,301 --> 00:27:27,800
Next the new stainless steel undergoes a treatment called annealing.

356
00:27:27,801 --> 00:27:34,100
The steel is heated and cooled to relieve internal stresses and make the metal less brittle.

357
00:27:34,101 --> 00:27:39,300
After that, an acid treatment called pickling removes any surface contaminants.

358
00:27:39,301 --> 00:27:46,800
Finally, the stainless steel is cleaned, polished, and cut to length before heading to fabricators

359
00:27:46,801 --> 00:27:51,680
across the country.

360
00:27:51,681 --> 00:27:57,340
Flatware made from stainless steel coils is a billion-dollar industry.

361
00:27:57,341 --> 00:28:03,420
At Sherrill Manufacturing in Sherrill, New York, the coils become more than 50,000 forks,

362
00:28:03,421 --> 00:28:05,980
spoons, and knives every day.

363
00:28:05,981 --> 00:28:09,660
Though it's the only flatware manufacturer left in the United States,

364
00:28:10,120 --> 00:28:13,060
it's one of the most technologically sophisticated in the world.

365
00:28:15,120 --> 00:28:19,540
Any flatware you see that has USA on the back stamp, it was made in this factory.

366
00:28:19,840 --> 00:28:25,420
We have a lot of automated equipment that allows us to use a lot less labor per piece.

367
00:28:25,720 --> 00:28:30,280
If this factory was running at full capacity, we could do about 400,000 pieces in a day.

368
00:28:30,281 --> 00:28:36,920
To make a fork, Sherrill typically uses 18-8 stainless steel.

369
00:28:37,820 --> 00:28:42,300
That means the steel contains 18% chrome and 8% nickel.

370
00:28:42,301 --> 00:28:44,920
The nickel increases the corrosion resistance.

371
00:28:45,420 --> 00:28:47,300
It also makes the part easier to manufacture.

372
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:50,420
It's softer. It doesn't harden as fast.

373
00:28:50,880 --> 00:28:55,440
When you have 18% chrome and no nickel, the part becomes very brittle.

374
00:28:55,441 --> 00:29:05,900
The manufacturing process starts by feeding a 650-foot-long stainless steel coil through a 150-ton blanking press.

375
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:10,419
This is the piece that comes out. It's a fork blank.

376
00:29:10,420 --> 00:29:14,040
The thickness of the material is 105 thousandths of an inch.

377
00:29:14,400 --> 00:29:17,114
From this coil, we can get about 16,000 forks.

378
00:29:17,115 --> 00:29:21,420
We'll go through about three of these coils a day, which is 7,500 pounds.

379
00:29:21,421 --> 00:29:28,420
After the press, the flat forks continue on to get their tines or prongs.

380
00:29:28,421 --> 00:29:35,420
To prevent the resulting prongs from bending, a so-called tine bar holds them together.

381
00:29:35,421 --> 00:29:40,420
Then we'll go into a stamping process where we put the pattern into the handle,

382
00:29:40,421 --> 00:29:43,420
and then the machine next to that will put the bowl on it.

383
00:29:43,421 --> 00:29:47,420
We strike the handle and raise the bowl in a coining press.

384
00:29:47,421 --> 00:29:50,420
It's very similar to making a coin, only a lot slower.

385
00:29:50,421 --> 00:29:55,420
Those pieces run about 5,000 pieces an hour.

386
00:29:55,421 --> 00:30:04,420
Once shaped, machines remove the tine bar and delicately buff the prongs.

387
00:30:04,421 --> 00:30:10,420
Then the forks pass through an eight-step buffing and polishing machine.

388
00:30:10,421 --> 00:30:16,420
After the pieces are buffed, they come to this machine here behind me, which is an ultrasonic wash.

389
00:30:16,421 --> 00:30:20,260
It uses detergent and ultrasonic waves to clean them.

390
00:30:21,360 --> 00:30:24,060
And then they're able to go into a bag nice and clean for the customer.

391
00:30:25,180 --> 00:30:28,900
So our homes are filled with chrome, sometimes by the bag full.

392
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:36,740
In 2000, the Sherrill Manufacturing Facility produced about 170 million pieces of stainless steel flatware.

393
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:44,080
By 2008, due to overseas competitors, that number had dropped to only 10 million pieces.

394
00:30:44,081 --> 00:30:47,920
Chrome will return on Modern Marvels.

395
00:30:49,080 --> 00:30:52,340
We now return to Chrome on Modern Marvels.

396
00:30:55,300 --> 00:30:56,500
The Chrysler Building.

397
00:30:58,200 --> 00:31:01,020
Thanks to Chrome, the eight-decade-old building

398
00:31:01,021 --> 00:31:03,660
is one of the most recognized skyscrapers in the world.

399
00:31:04,360 --> 00:31:06,620
The Chrome gives it that brilliance, the shininess,

400
00:31:06,920 --> 00:31:08,860
and that is one of the most extraordinary things

401
00:31:08,861 --> 00:31:10,160
about the top of the Chrysler Building.

402
00:31:10,320 --> 00:31:12,260
On a day like today, with the sun shining,

403
00:31:12,261 --> 00:31:16,160
It glints off of the metal, and it looks just stunning.

404
00:31:17,400 --> 00:31:21,280
In fact, the special stainless steel that glistens in the New York sun

405
00:31:21,281 --> 00:31:23,520
contains 18% chrome.

406
00:31:25,060 --> 00:31:29,180
And it was actually selected by Walter Chrysler himself

407
00:31:29,181 --> 00:31:31,200
for its performance characteristics,

408
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:34,580
and that it looked a lot like the chrome used on cars.

409
00:31:34,780 --> 00:31:37,820
He had it tested at his laboratories in Michigan

410
00:31:37,821 --> 00:31:41,180
and found that it was going to perform very well.

411
00:31:42,260 --> 00:31:48,220
The automobile tycoon chose the so-called Nyrosta metal due to the fact

412
00:31:48,221 --> 00:31:52,160
that it didn't rust and never had to be cleaned or polished to maintain its

413
00:31:52,161 --> 00:31:59,900
luster. Created in the 1920s by Krupp Metalwork of Germany, Nyrosta was the

414
00:31:59,901 --> 00:32:05,600
first stainless steel used in a major architectural project. The use of this

415
00:32:05,601 --> 00:32:09,720
chrome heavy metal, along with the sleek and modern design of the dome and spire,

416
00:32:09,721 --> 00:32:13,760
was very characteristic of the Art Deco design movement of the time.

417
00:32:15,460 --> 00:32:19,100
This was a period, you know, the whole idea of we're in the modern world.

418
00:32:19,440 --> 00:32:23,720
Let's have modern styles. Let's have forward-looking things.

419
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:30,340
Art Deco takes its name from a design exposition held in Paris in 1925.

420
00:32:31,740 --> 00:32:37,040
American consumers found the geometric style of metallic materials elegant and functional.

421
00:32:37,041 --> 00:32:44,040
Soon designers were using chrome on everything, from furniture to toasters.

422
00:32:44,041 --> 00:32:52,040
And today, the Chrysler Building remains the most famous Art Deco skyscraper.

423
00:32:52,041 --> 00:33:02,040
Those receding circles rising up with the triangular windows and merging into that 185-foot spire rising into the sky, you can't miss it.

424
00:33:02,041 --> 00:33:03,040
You can't miss it.

425
00:33:03,041 --> 00:33:08,280
Add to that all of the decorative features, the metal, it's got all of the geometric effects.

426
00:33:08,281 --> 00:33:09,500
There's just nothing quite like it.

427
00:33:09,501 --> 00:33:14,060
It is the Art Deco skyscraper.

428
00:33:14,061 --> 00:33:18,840
Construction started in 1928 and took just 18 months.

429
00:33:18,841 --> 00:33:24,120
In a shop set up inside the building, metal workers measured and cut the nyroste.

430
00:33:24,121 --> 00:33:29,700
Then roofers attached and joined the shiny metal together piece by piece to create the

431
00:33:29,701 --> 00:33:37,620
entire facade above the 61st floor it was all custom and fabricated work

432
00:33:37,621 --> 00:33:43,020
the famous pineapples radiator caps and eagles were fashioned by hand from

433
00:33:43,021 --> 00:33:50,220
Nyro stuff and then attached to iron frames protruding from the building

434
00:33:50,221 --> 00:33:56,740
these are what we called the flying radiator caps or wings of mercury but

435
00:33:56,741 --> 00:34:01,580
It's basically a replica of the 1929 radiator cap from a Chrysler.

436
00:34:01,581 --> 00:34:07,580
Looking at this piece, it probably came as a hemisphere on that disc,

437
00:34:07,581 --> 00:34:09,920
a hemisphere on this disc.

438
00:34:09,921 --> 00:34:12,260
This would have been a separate piece.

439
00:34:12,261 --> 00:34:15,760
Then that crowning element may have been the last part.

440
00:34:17,300 --> 00:34:20,600
The automobile iconography is throughout the building,

441
00:34:20,601 --> 00:34:24,260
but here, especially on the 31st floor, it's really apparent.

442
00:34:24,261 --> 00:34:27,700
You start with a radiator cap, much larger scale,

443
00:34:27,701 --> 00:34:29,820
and then down over on the wall here,

444
00:34:30,180 --> 00:34:35,340
you can see there's a replication of the wheels and the hubcaps from a Chrysler.

445
00:34:35,720 --> 00:34:38,300
So they're much larger than they are in the car,

446
00:34:38,520 --> 00:34:44,100
but they're integrated into the architecture in a very creative and frankly fun way.

447
00:34:46,300 --> 00:34:49,880
Stainless steel also cloaks the 185-foot Vertex.

448
00:34:49,881 --> 00:34:55,980
But the sparkling needle piercing the sky wasn't always part of Chrysler's design.

449
00:34:57,080 --> 00:35:00,400
In fact, the structure was added solely to make the skyscraper taller.

450
00:35:02,440 --> 00:35:06,840
That enormous piece of steel that you see rising at the very top

451
00:35:06,841 --> 00:35:10,060
was brought to the building secretly in five pieces,

452
00:35:10,460 --> 00:35:14,480
welded together in the dark of night, and then as dawn broke,

453
00:35:15,060 --> 00:35:17,880
it rose with a winch right up through the top,

454
00:35:17,881 --> 00:35:21,560
bringing the height of the building to 1,046 feet

455
00:35:21,561 --> 00:35:23,980
and claiming the title of world's tallest building,

456
00:35:24,420 --> 00:35:26,560
which it held for almost 18 months

457
